   482	### B.5 The reconstruction that is *not* a bound (the FC5 miss that was checked)
   483	
   484	Prop 3’s child frame is not constant: \(\bar\kappa_1=6A-1\),
   485	\(\nu_1=N=9A-2\), \(d_p=3N\), \(d_q=2N+1\) all scale with \(A\).
   486	In particular \(A=(\bar\kappa_1+1)/6\) is a rational function of the
   487	child frame. If a later theorem bounded that frame, it would bound
   488	pole mass. **FC5 does not emit the frame; it consumes the frame and
   489	emits \((w,M)\).** Depth-closure does not bound \(\kappa_i\)
   490	(`SHEET6-DEPTH.md:128-138`). NF-M’s locality theorem requires the
   491	same type *and the same emitted frame* (`NF-M.md:108-116`); the BB2
   492	cylinder is the exact quantifier witness: one type `b=-a` for every
   493	\(N\), infinitely many tagged \((N,\mathrm{type},\mathrm{frame})\)
   494	triples, constant FC5 pair \((6,1)\). Sol §8 is the right answer to
   495	“use the frame, not just \((w,M)\).” There is no present theorem
   496	that bounds the frame parameters at \(M\ge 2\).
   497	
   498	Depth-closure cannot restore coercivity for four independent reasons,
   499	all printed in `SHEET6-DEPTH.md` and `SHEET6-DEPTH-REVIEW.md`: scope
   500	is the `M=1` axis; \(W(w_0)\) is per fixed entry; \(\kappa_i\) is
   501	uncontrolled; the representative bound is on the cumulative jump-cell
   502	menu, not on endpoint frames. Prop 3 lives on \(M=2\to 3\).
   503	
   504	NF-M cannot restore coercivity: \((\widehat Q-1)!\) counts decorations
   505	of a *fixed* zero-dimensional schema. Prop 3 has \(\widehat Q=2\)
   506	and one type for every \(A\). The unbounded parameter is the schema
   507	itself.
